Instead of copying unique values forward, this one removes duplicate entries as they are found. When two neighboring values match, `splice` deletes the later one. The index does not move in that case, because a new value has shifted into the same position.

This is easy to follow, but it is slower for large arrays because deleting from the middle shifts later elements. The input array is changed directly.

```javascript
class Solution {
    removeDuplicates(nums) {
        let index = 1;

        while (index < nums.length) {
            if (nums[index] === nums[index - 1]) {
                nums.splice(index, 1);
            } else {
                index++;
            }
        }

        return nums.length;
    }
}
```

A dry run for `[2, 2, 2, 3]` removes the second `2`, checks the new second `2` again, removes it, and then moves past `3`. The final list is `[2, 3]`.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because each splice can shift many values.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the internal work done by the array operation.

The standard library already has an algorithm for moving consecutive duplicates to the end of a sorted range. `std::unique` returns an iterator to the first position after the kept values, so the distance from the beginning is the new length.

Only the first returned length of `nums` is relevant. The vector is modified in place, although its physical size is not reduced.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {
        auto newEnd = unique(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        return static_cast<int>(distance(nums.begin(), newEnd));
    }
};
```

For `[1, 1, 2, 3, 3]`, `std::unique` moves the kept values to the front as `[1, 2, 3, ...]`, and the returned distance is `3`.

An empty vector also works: both iterators are equal, so the result is `0`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The sorted order makes the check simple. The first value stays in place, and `write` marks where the next new value should go. Every value after `write` can be ignored by the judge.

For example, with `[0, 0, 1, 1, 2]`, the scan keeps `0`, skips the second `0`, then writes `1` and `2` into the next open positions. The input list is changed in place.

```python
class Solution:
    def removeDuplicates(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:
        if not nums:
            return 0

        write = 1
        for read in range(1, len(nums)):
            if nums[read] != nums[write - 1]:
                nums[write] = nums[read]
                write += 1

        return write
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the list is scanned once.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

Since equal values are next to each other, there is no need to search the whole prefix for every number. `read` checks each position, while `write` points to the next place for a distinct value.

The empty array is handled before reading the first element. For a one-element array, the loop does nothing and the method returns `1`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {
        if (nums.length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int write = 1;
        for (int read = 1; read < nums.length; read++) {
            if (nums[read] != nums[read - 1]) {
                nums[write] = nums[read];
                write++;
            }
        }

        return write;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

